Thanks for coming.

00:00:08.950 --> 00:00:10.380
My name's Manish.

00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:13.020
I'm a product manager on
Google Compute Engine.

00:00:13.020 --> 00:00:15.180
Today, we're going to be
talking about saving money

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:16.650
on Compute Engine.

00:00:16.650 --> 00:00:18.150
Before we dive into
the topic, there

00:00:18.150 --> 00:00:19.833
is a Dory for this session.

00:00:19.833 --> 00:00:21.250
My amazing colleague
Scott's going

00:00:21.250 --> 00:00:23.292
to be moderating it, so
if any questions pop up

00:00:23.292 --> 00:00:24.750
while we're going
through the talk,

00:00:24.750 --> 00:00:26.610
feel free to post
those questions there.

00:00:26.610 --> 00:00:27.900
And we'll answer some
of those questions,

00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:30.030
as well as take some live
questions at these remotes

00:00:30.030 --> 00:00:31.113
at the end of the session.

00:00:33.580 --> 00:00:37.270
So wasting money on cloud
is a very real problem.

00:00:37.270 --> 00:00:40.762
Our friends at Rightscale
do an annual survey.

00:00:40.762 --> 00:00:42.220
Basically every
year, they do this.

00:00:42.220 --> 00:00:47.770
And just like last year,
they found that 35% of cloud

00:00:47.770 --> 00:00:50.710
spend is being lost.

00:00:50.710 --> 00:00:52.190
That's a very large number.

00:00:52.190 --> 00:00:54.730
And what's causing it is a host
of different things, right?

00:00:54.730 --> 00:00:57.610
It may be that you're running
instances or resources that you

00:00:57.610 --> 00:00:59.260
no longer need anymore.

00:00:59.260 --> 00:01:01.300
Or you've over
provisioned resources,

00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:04.030
or you just haven't bought the
right discounts for the things

00:01:04.030 --> 00:01:05.510
that you are running today.

00:01:05.510 --> 00:01:08.050
But regardless, 35%
over the past two years

00:01:08.050 --> 00:01:11.330
straight is a pretty big number.

00:01:11.330 --> 00:01:13.760
And it's not because
customers don't care.

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:17.020
The majority of
respondents in the survey

00:01:17.020 --> 00:01:22.450
cite cloud optimization
cloud cost as a top concern.

00:01:22.450 --> 00:01:24.730
Yet the number is still so big.

00:01:24.730 --> 00:01:27.430
And the reason for that
is it's not easy, right?

00:01:27.430 --> 00:01:29.118
Cloud is a really dynamic place.

00:01:29.118 --> 00:01:30.410
Things are constantly changing.

00:01:30.410 --> 00:01:32.743
There's new features coming
to Compute Engine every week

00:01:32.743 --> 00:01:34.000
or every month.

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:35.470
And then your
actual environment,

00:01:35.470 --> 00:01:38.770
you have resources coming and
going at whatever churn rate.

00:01:38.770 --> 00:01:40.560
You have no idea
[INAUDIBLE] times.

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:43.150
So it can be really
hard to keep up.

00:01:43.150 --> 00:01:46.030
So in today's talk, what
we're going to focus on

00:01:46.030 --> 00:01:48.760
are what are some of
the common patterns

00:01:48.760 --> 00:01:50.860
for waste in terms
of cloud spend

00:01:50.860 --> 00:01:53.510
and what you can do about it.

00:01:53.510 --> 00:01:56.120
We'll tackle that
in four section.

00:01:56.120 --> 00:01:57.970
The first, we'll
talk about how Google

00:01:57.970 --> 00:02:00.350
thinks about pricing products.

00:02:00.350 --> 00:02:04.000
The second is we'll
talk about understanding

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:06.460
what are the resources that
you're actually paying for.

00:02:06.460 --> 00:02:08.960
If you don't understand what
the resources you're paying for

00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:10.957
are, it's really hard
to optimize those costs.

00:02:10.957 --> 00:02:12.790
And then we'll talk
about those common areas

00:02:12.790 --> 00:02:15.940
where waste happens and
what are tactical things you

00:02:15.940 --> 00:02:19.150
can do to remove that waste.

00:02:19.150 --> 00:02:21.130
And then finally, we'll
close on optimizing

00:02:21.130 --> 00:02:22.870
the types of
discounts you can make

00:02:22.870 --> 00:02:25.900
or purchase to help optimize
the actual resources that you're

00:02:25.900 --> 00:02:27.830
running.

00:02:27.830 --> 00:02:28.780
So let's get started.

00:02:28.780 --> 00:02:30.700
We'll talk about pricing
principles first.

00:02:30.700 --> 00:02:35.720
So when we at Google Cloud
think about pricing products,

00:02:35.720 --> 00:02:38.990
we try to keep three
principles in mind--

00:02:38.990 --> 00:02:42.527
simple, flexible, and fair.

00:02:42.527 --> 00:02:44.860
Now, these principles can
mean a lot of different things

00:02:44.860 --> 00:02:45.710
to different people.

00:02:45.710 --> 00:02:47.920
So let me talk through
each one of these

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:51.350
and describe what
it means to us.

00:02:51.350 --> 00:02:56.420
Simple means powerful enough to
meet your needs without being

00:02:56.420 --> 00:02:59.143
unnecessarily complex.

00:02:59.143 --> 00:03:01.310
And so that doesn't mean
that a five-year-old should

00:03:01.310 --> 00:03:03.265
be able to understand
your cloud bill,

00:03:03.265 --> 00:03:04.640
but it also doesn't
mean that you

00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:08.090
should be required to have a
PhD in finance to figure out

00:03:08.090 --> 00:03:09.170
your cloud bill.

00:03:09.170 --> 00:03:10.960
And so one of the
features that we

00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:13.460
have that we launched last year
is something called resource

00:03:13.460 --> 00:03:14.300
based pricing.

00:03:14.300 --> 00:03:18.170
Resource based pricing basically
charges or meters usage

00:03:18.170 --> 00:03:20.990
against each resource
as an independent SKU.

00:03:20.990 --> 00:03:22.985
So it's super easy
to figure out where

00:03:22.985 --> 00:03:24.110
you're spending your money.

00:03:24.110 --> 00:03:25.777
So you could look at
it and say, oh, I'm

00:03:25.777 --> 00:03:28.340
spending x dollars on
cores, y dollars on RAM,

00:03:28.340 --> 00:03:30.840
and some money on some
premium OS licenses.

00:03:30.840 --> 00:03:33.530
So there's no weird bundles
to sort of reverse engineer.

00:03:33.530 --> 00:03:35.018
There's no hidden costs.

00:03:35.018 --> 00:03:37.310
It makes things super simple,
and easy, and transparent

00:03:37.310 --> 00:03:40.530
to figure out what's
going on your bill.

00:03:40.530 --> 00:03:41.838
Flexible.

00:03:41.838 --> 00:03:42.380
So we get it.

00:03:42.380 --> 00:03:44.400
Things change over time.

00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:47.870
And you shouldn't be scared to
make a decision today and be

00:03:47.870 --> 00:03:51.090
punished if things
change in the future.

00:03:51.090 --> 00:03:53.090
So this is something that
we spend a lot of time

00:03:53.090 --> 00:03:54.230
on with our pricing models.

00:03:54.230 --> 00:03:56.750
We think really hard about
how do we make things flexible

00:03:56.750 --> 00:04:00.260
enough so that our pricing
models can evolve and adapt

00:04:00.260 --> 00:04:05.572
to your evolving workloads
and your changing workloads.

00:04:05.572 --> 00:04:08.030
One example of this is something
that we call committed use

00:04:08.030 --> 00:04:08.630
discounts.

00:04:08.630 --> 00:04:10.430
And the idea with
committed use discounts

00:04:10.430 --> 00:04:16.220
is you purchase an
aggregate commitment

00:04:16.220 --> 00:04:18.440
for x amount of
resources, say, 1,000

00:04:18.440 --> 00:04:20.503
cores in a specific region.

00:04:20.503 --> 00:04:21.920
And in exchange,
you say I'm going

00:04:21.920 --> 00:04:23.940
to pay for that for
one or three years.

00:04:23.940 --> 00:04:26.300
And you can get some fairly
deep discounts this way.

00:04:26.300 --> 00:04:29.090
But what makes committed
use discounts flexible

00:04:29.090 --> 00:04:31.850
is that we're not asking
you for a specific machine

00:04:31.850 --> 00:04:35.960
shape, or a specific zone,
or a specific operating

00:04:35.960 --> 00:04:38.512
system, or network type,
or anything like that.

00:04:38.512 --> 00:04:40.970
You're just telling us what
region and the amount of cores.

00:04:40.970 --> 00:04:43.320
And now you can run
whatever VMs you want.

00:04:43.320 --> 00:04:44.390
So VMs can come and go.

00:04:44.390 --> 00:04:45.435
You can change shapes.

00:04:45.435 --> 00:04:47.060
You can change zones
within the region.

00:04:47.060 --> 00:04:48.910
You can change
operating systems.

00:04:48.910 --> 00:04:50.660
Those commitments that
you made on day one

00:04:50.660 --> 00:04:52.350
will continue to apply.

00:04:52.350 --> 00:04:54.320
So the whole goal here
is that you shouldn't

00:04:54.320 --> 00:04:56.600
have to feel afraid to make
some of these commitments

00:04:56.600 --> 00:04:59.472
on our platform.

00:04:59.472 --> 00:05:00.430
And then finally, fair.

00:05:00.430 --> 00:05:07.270
Fair first and foremost means
a low competitive price always.

00:05:07.270 --> 00:05:09.820
What it also means, though,
is that if we see that you're

00:05:09.820 --> 00:05:11.330
wasting money in some way--

00:05:11.330 --> 00:05:14.050
for example, if you're running
an over provisioned instance

00:05:14.050 --> 00:05:15.160
and we notice it--

00:05:15.160 --> 00:05:16.610
we're going to get in your face.

00:05:16.610 --> 00:05:18.110
And we're going to
tell you about it

00:05:18.110 --> 00:05:20.110
so that you can take action.

00:05:20.110 --> 00:05:22.780
We call that instance
right sizing.

00:05:22.780 --> 00:05:25.060
Another thing that
we do or what's

00:05:25.060 --> 00:05:27.400
sort of driven from
our fairness ideal

00:05:27.400 --> 00:05:31.490
is if you happen to do
something with your workload

00:05:31.490 --> 00:05:33.560
and it saves Google
money, it's only fair

00:05:33.560 --> 00:05:36.050
that we pass those
savings on to you.

00:05:36.050 --> 00:05:39.920
So for example, if you run a
resource for an entire month,

00:05:39.920 --> 00:05:42.063
that gives efficiency to Google.

00:05:42.063 --> 00:05:44.480
And so Google should pass some
of those savings on to you.

00:05:44.480 --> 00:05:46.710
And so we do exactly that with
something called sustained use

00:05:46.710 --> 00:05:47.480
discounts.

00:05:47.480 --> 00:05:48.620
You don't need to
tell us anything.

00:05:48.620 --> 00:05:50.000
You just run your
workload, and if you

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:52.520
happen to run it for some
steady state within the month,

00:05:52.520 --> 00:05:55.250
you'll automatically
get discounts.

00:05:55.250 --> 00:05:58.833
And this commitment to
simplicity, flexibility,

00:05:58.833 --> 00:06:01.250
and fairness is not something
that you find with all cloud

00:06:01.250 --> 00:06:02.833
providers and
definitely not something

00:06:02.833 --> 00:06:07.790
that you've seen from all
cloud providers from day one.

00:06:07.790 --> 00:06:09.530
So let's move on to
the second section.

00:06:09.530 --> 00:06:11.750
We're going to start talking
about understanding what

00:06:11.750 --> 00:06:14.333
are you actually getting charged
for in terms of the resources

00:06:14.333 --> 00:06:16.840
that you run within
Compute Engine.

00:06:16.840 --> 00:06:19.190
So at a high level,
you can group

00:06:19.190 --> 00:06:21.620
the resources into
two buckets, the base

00:06:21.620 --> 00:06:25.710
VM, which is the cores and RAM,
and the attachable resources.

00:06:25.710 --> 00:06:28.303
So these are things like GPUs,
persistent disk, licensing,

00:06:28.303 --> 00:06:29.720
and there's a whole
bunch of other

00:06:29.720 --> 00:06:32.480
little-- not little-- but
other resources like local SSD.

00:06:42.560 --> 00:06:45.500
So one of the things
here is one of the core

00:06:45.500 --> 00:06:48.800
things around Compute Engine
is providing visibility

00:06:48.800 --> 00:06:50.730
into the resources
that you're running.

00:06:50.730 --> 00:06:52.730
And so when you're trying
to optimize your cost,

00:06:52.730 --> 00:06:55.890
you want to understand where
you're spending your money.

00:06:55.890 --> 00:06:58.070
So a lot of Compute
Engine customers spend--

00:06:58.070 --> 00:07:00.470
most of their spend ends up
on their base VM, the cores

00:07:00.470 --> 00:07:01.453
and RAM.

00:07:01.453 --> 00:07:02.870
But there are
attachable resources

00:07:02.870 --> 00:07:06.750
that can be quite expensive,
like a V100 GPU, for example.

00:07:06.750 --> 00:07:11.210
And so optimizing your GPU
spend can also have a material

00:07:11.210 --> 00:07:13.130
impact on your overall bill.

00:07:13.130 --> 00:07:15.380
And so with resource based
pricing, which is something

00:07:15.380 --> 00:07:18.225
that we talked about
a little bit earlier,

00:07:18.225 --> 00:07:20.600
it's really easy to get a
picture of what you're actually

00:07:20.600 --> 00:07:21.570
spending your money on.

00:07:21.570 --> 00:07:23.330
And so you can kind of
stack rank and focus,

00:07:23.330 --> 00:07:25.790
where do I need to spend the
most attention initially, which

00:07:25.790 --> 00:07:26.957
could be your cores and RAM.

00:07:26.957 --> 00:07:28.990
Or if you're a
heavy GPU user, you

00:07:28.990 --> 00:07:32.780
may be wanting to
optimize your GPUs.

00:07:32.780 --> 00:07:36.392
So how do you figure out what's
actually running right now?

00:07:36.392 --> 00:07:37.850
So if you want to
understand what's

00:07:37.850 --> 00:07:39.980
happening at a point
in time right now,

00:07:39.980 --> 00:07:42.620
you can take advantage
of our instance list

00:07:42.620 --> 00:07:44.450
and describe APIs.

00:07:44.450 --> 00:07:47.630
So with the List API, you get
a list of all of the instances

00:07:47.630 --> 00:07:51.415
that are currently
running on your project.

00:07:51.415 --> 00:07:52.790
And then you can
use the instance

00:07:52.790 --> 00:07:56.190
Describe API to see what
exactly that instance is.

00:07:56.190 --> 00:07:57.740
And so the cost
driving fields here

00:07:57.740 --> 00:07:59.810
are things like
machine type, right?

00:07:59.810 --> 00:08:02.750
So this is the base
VM, the core and RAM.

00:08:02.750 --> 00:08:05.430
There's also accelerator type
and accelerator accounts.

00:08:05.430 --> 00:08:07.610
So these are the GPUs
that are running,

00:08:07.610 --> 00:08:10.070
and the number of GPUs,
and the type of GPU.

00:08:10.070 --> 00:08:11.038
There's your disk.

00:08:11.038 --> 00:08:12.830
And so there could be
multiple disk objects

00:08:12.830 --> 00:08:14.450
within that resource in the API.

00:08:14.450 --> 00:08:16.970
So you could have persistent
disk, multiple persistent disks

00:08:16.970 --> 00:08:20.870
attached to your VM, or you
could have a local SSD disk

00:08:20.870 --> 00:08:21.370
as well.

00:08:21.370 --> 00:08:23.935
And local SSD can also be a
fairly expensive resource.

00:08:23.935 --> 00:08:25.310
And then you can
also take a look

00:08:25.310 --> 00:08:27.950
at what's running in terms of
the licensing or the premium

00:08:27.950 --> 00:08:30.350
OS's attached to the
VMs that you're running.

00:08:33.520 --> 00:08:37.500
So, seeing what's running at
sort of that moment in time

00:08:37.500 --> 00:08:42.330
is useful, but a lot of
cloud environments, what's

00:08:42.330 --> 00:08:44.130
running right now is
not indicative of what

00:08:44.130 --> 00:08:46.710
ran four months ago or
over that period of time

00:08:46.710 --> 00:08:48.930
between four months
ago and today.

00:08:48.930 --> 00:08:51.210
And so for that type
of historical analysis,

00:08:51.210 --> 00:08:52.980
we have a really,
really awesome tool.

00:08:52.980 --> 00:08:55.290
It's called BigQuery
Billing export.

00:08:55.290 --> 00:08:58.200
Once you enable this export,
what basically happens

00:08:58.200 --> 00:09:03.137
is all the usage that is
charge against your project

00:09:03.137 --> 00:09:05.220
and all the different SKUs
will get logged and put

00:09:05.220 --> 00:09:07.430
into a BigQuery data set.

00:09:07.430 --> 00:09:11.115
And then you can do analysis on
that data set for whatever time

00:09:11.115 --> 00:09:12.018
frame you want.

00:09:12.018 --> 00:09:13.560
The main thing that
I would point out

00:09:13.560 --> 00:09:16.090
is that it's not
sort of retroactive.

00:09:16.090 --> 00:09:18.120
So once you enable it,
that is when the data

00:09:18.120 --> 00:09:19.288
will start getting stored.

00:09:19.288 --> 00:09:21.330
So you're not going to
get historical data if you

00:09:21.330 --> 00:09:23.377
haven't activated it yet.

00:09:23.377 --> 00:09:24.960
And so the idea here
is really simple.

00:09:24.960 --> 00:09:27.120
It's a really simple
example that's up here.

00:09:27.120 --> 00:09:29.220
You can ask the
data set to give you

00:09:29.220 --> 00:09:31.440
all of the SKUs
associated with Compute

00:09:31.440 --> 00:09:35.790
Engine for a specific project,
or for a billing account,

00:09:35.790 --> 00:09:38.040
or multiple projects
if you want.

00:09:38.040 --> 00:09:40.830
And then you group
by the SKU name.

00:09:40.830 --> 00:09:42.420
And then sum up
the cost, and then

00:09:42.420 --> 00:09:45.150
sort in descending
order by the cost.

00:09:45.150 --> 00:09:47.220
And what that will give
you in terms of an export

00:09:47.220 --> 00:09:50.580
is exactly all of the specific
resources that you're paying,

00:09:50.580 --> 00:09:53.280
from the most expensive on your
bill all the way to your least

00:09:53.280 --> 00:09:54.610
expensive.

00:09:54.610 --> 00:09:57.390
So in this example, it's a
bunch of N1 standard cores,

00:09:57.390 --> 00:10:02.310
some N1 standard RAM, and then
there's some V100 GPUs running.

00:10:02.310 --> 00:10:04.090
So this is a really,
really powerful tool,

00:10:04.090 --> 00:10:07.072
so something I really encourage
you folks to turn on when you

00:10:07.072 --> 00:10:08.280
start creating your products.

00:10:08.280 --> 00:10:10.570
Or if you already have
a project, turn it on.

00:10:10.570 --> 00:10:13.087
It's a great way to sort
of analyze what's happening

00:10:13.087 --> 00:10:14.670
and assess what you
should do in terms

00:10:14.670 --> 00:10:18.180
of optimizing your cloud costs.

00:10:18.180 --> 00:10:21.432
So now I want to introduce
a fictional company.

00:10:21.432 --> 00:10:23.640
I took some of the usage
patterns from a real company

00:10:23.640 --> 00:10:24.473
and applied it here.

00:10:24.473 --> 00:10:26.490
But this is not a real company.

00:10:26.490 --> 00:10:30.030
It's Example Inc. They just
ramped up on Compute Engine.

00:10:30.030 --> 00:10:33.540
They're spending about
$1.4 million a year now.

00:10:33.540 --> 00:10:37.480
They've done no work to
optimize their bills.

00:10:37.480 --> 00:10:38.080
So nothing.

00:10:38.080 --> 00:10:39.690
They just started
creating instances.

00:10:39.690 --> 00:10:42.460
So there's probably a lot
of low hanging fruit here.

00:10:42.460 --> 00:10:44.880
And the purpose of
this example company

00:10:44.880 --> 00:10:47.280
is to help illustrate
some of what

00:10:47.280 --> 00:10:50.240
an optimization can
do on the overall bill

00:10:50.240 --> 00:10:52.212
for a semi-real example.

00:10:52.212 --> 00:10:54.420
And just to make sure that
we're all kind of aligned,

00:10:54.420 --> 00:10:56.160
so they're spending 1.4 million.

00:10:56.160 --> 00:10:58.230
They've got a whole
bunch of cores.

00:10:58.230 --> 00:11:01.680
So they have 230 VMs running
across different VM shapes.

00:11:01.680 --> 00:11:04.530
They've got some GPUs,
just eight V100s.

00:11:04.530 --> 00:11:08.970
They've got about 120
terabytes of PD SSD.

00:11:08.970 --> 00:11:11.700
And they're also running
some Windows workloads.

00:11:14.950 --> 00:11:19.450
So let's take Example Inc on
the journey of doing some cost

00:11:19.450 --> 00:11:20.230
optimization.

00:11:24.490 --> 00:11:27.420
So the first thing we
would say to Example Inc

00:11:27.420 --> 00:11:32.530
is pick the right size for
your workload, instance size

00:11:32.530 --> 00:11:33.850
for your workload.

00:11:33.850 --> 00:11:36.880
And Compute Engine offers
a great deal of flexibility

00:11:36.880 --> 00:11:40.360
here with something that we
call custom machine shapes.

00:11:40.360 --> 00:11:42.910
And a lot of other
cloud providers,

00:11:42.910 --> 00:11:46.780
what they require you to do
is choose between a fixed list

00:11:46.780 --> 00:11:48.882
of predefined machine shapes.

00:11:48.882 --> 00:11:50.590
And sometimes what
that means is that you

00:11:50.590 --> 00:11:52.630
have to pick a
machine that is bigger

00:11:52.630 --> 00:11:57.100
than what you need in terms
of cores, RAM, or local SSD.

00:11:57.100 --> 00:11:59.020
That's basically waste.

00:11:59.020 --> 00:12:01.300
With custom machine
types, what you can do

00:12:01.300 --> 00:12:03.850
is pick the right
amount of cores,

00:12:03.850 --> 00:12:06.070
the right amount
of memory, and not

00:12:06.070 --> 00:12:08.860
have to overpay for
the cores and RAM

00:12:08.860 --> 00:12:12.520
that you would have to do with
the predefined machine type.

00:12:12.520 --> 00:12:15.590
So a couple of caveats
with custom machine types.

00:12:15.590 --> 00:12:17.158
There are some restrictions.

00:12:17.158 --> 00:12:18.700
So if you want a
custom machine type,

00:12:18.700 --> 00:12:21.560
you have to do it in
increments of two vCPUs--

00:12:21.560 --> 00:12:24.410
so two, four, six, eight--

00:12:24.410 --> 00:12:28.690
and then 0.25
gigabytes of memory.

00:12:28.690 --> 00:12:31.810
Even with those sort of
caveats or restrictions,

00:12:31.810 --> 00:12:33.730
custom machine types
are significantly more

00:12:33.730 --> 00:12:35.590
flexible than most
predefined machine types

00:12:35.590 --> 00:12:36.440
that you see out there.

00:12:36.440 --> 00:12:37.815
So there's real
cost savings here

00:12:37.815 --> 00:12:41.150
if you leverage
custom machine types.

00:12:41.150 --> 00:12:43.750
So if you have VMs
running right now

00:12:43.750 --> 00:12:47.090
or you're going to be launching
instances in the future,

00:12:47.090 --> 00:12:48.340
think about right sizing them.

00:12:48.340 --> 00:12:49.965
You may be over
provisioning these VMs,

00:12:49.965 --> 00:12:54.250
and there's significant savings
here for you potentially.

00:12:54.250 --> 00:12:58.990
So generally, most
predefined machine types

00:12:58.990 --> 00:13:01.300
are in shapes of what
we call powers of 2.

00:13:01.300 --> 00:13:06.873
And so that's 2,
4, 8, 16, 32, 64.

00:13:06.873 --> 00:13:08.290
And the reason
that is, is that it

00:13:08.290 --> 00:13:10.880
makes bin packing on the
cloud provider side really,

00:13:10.880 --> 00:13:12.400
really easy.

00:13:12.400 --> 00:13:14.890
So it's great for the provider,
but it's not great for you

00:13:14.890 --> 00:13:17.410
because you're forced to
pay for resources that you

00:13:17.410 --> 00:13:19.640
don't actually need.

00:13:19.640 --> 00:13:20.780
So let's take an example.

00:13:20.780 --> 00:13:22.700
Let's say you want to run--

00:13:22.700 --> 00:13:24.700
you need 20 cores.

00:13:24.700 --> 00:13:27.580
In the old powers of 2 world,
what you'd be stuck with

00:13:27.580 --> 00:13:30.100
is a 32 core VM.

00:13:30.100 --> 00:13:32.270
And that means you're
paying for 12 extra cores

00:13:32.270 --> 00:13:34.340
that you don't need.

00:13:34.340 --> 00:13:36.920
So that doesn't seem really
fair from our pricing principle

00:13:36.920 --> 00:13:38.380
perspective, right?

00:13:38.380 --> 00:13:40.130
So take advantage of
custom machine types.

00:13:40.130 --> 00:13:42.005
Take a look at what you
can do to shrink down

00:13:42.005 --> 00:13:44.280
your VMs to the right size.

00:13:44.280 --> 00:13:46.090
So in order to do
that actual step,

00:13:46.090 --> 00:13:47.720
there's a little
code snippet here.

00:13:47.720 --> 00:13:49.680
The idea here is you
stop the instance.

00:13:49.680 --> 00:13:51.380
So there's an outage.

00:13:51.380 --> 00:13:53.255
You would change
the shape of the VM,

00:13:53.255 --> 00:13:55.130
and then you would start
the instance backup.

00:13:55.130 --> 00:13:57.380
And it would reconnect all
your PDs and all of that.

00:13:57.380 --> 00:13:58.530
So there is an outage.

00:13:58.530 --> 00:14:01.310
So you want to be-- do it
during your maintenance windows,

00:14:01.310 --> 00:14:03.410
or if it's not
critical as a dev test,

00:14:03.410 --> 00:14:05.210
you can just do it
whenever it makes sense.

00:14:07.850 --> 00:14:11.940
So figuring out if you
are over provisioning

00:14:11.940 --> 00:14:14.860
an instance is not necessarily
a simple task, right?

00:14:14.860 --> 00:14:17.472
Workloads can change over time.

00:14:17.472 --> 00:14:19.180
So you'd have to have
a lot of telemetry.

00:14:19.180 --> 00:14:21.222
You'd be trying to see
what's happening to the VM

00:14:21.222 --> 00:14:24.700
and trying to figure out, oh,
should I right size it or not?

00:14:24.700 --> 00:14:27.850
So following our
principles around fairness,

00:14:27.850 --> 00:14:28.780
we watch instances.

00:14:28.780 --> 00:14:31.130
We see the utilization rates.

00:14:31.130 --> 00:14:33.340
And so if we notice
a VM that's being

00:14:33.340 --> 00:14:35.490
underutilized or
even over utilized,

00:14:35.490 --> 00:14:36.490
we're going to tell you.

00:14:36.490 --> 00:14:37.290
We're going to get in
your face, and we're

00:14:37.290 --> 00:14:38.520
going to tell you about it.

00:14:38.520 --> 00:14:40.820
And so we call this
rightsizing recommendations.

00:14:40.820 --> 00:14:42.400
This is available in GA today.

00:14:42.400 --> 00:14:44.440
You can just log into
the cloud console.

00:14:44.440 --> 00:14:47.800
Go to the instance, the
Compute Engine instance page.

00:14:47.800 --> 00:14:50.050
And you'll see a list of
recommendations there.

00:14:50.050 --> 00:14:52.030
And with a push of
a button, you can

00:14:52.030 --> 00:14:54.730
either accept or decline the
recommendation, and go ahead

00:14:54.730 --> 00:14:55.490
and do it.

00:14:55.490 --> 00:14:57.970
Again, there is an outage
here, so don't just

00:14:57.970 --> 00:14:59.620
start clicking things.

00:14:59.620 --> 00:15:02.230
But it's basically
propagated here,

00:15:02.230 --> 00:15:03.920
so you can see what's happening.

00:15:07.140 --> 00:15:09.938
So doing that is great.

00:15:09.938 --> 00:15:11.355
But it's still
very manual, right?

00:15:11.355 --> 00:15:14.663
So you have to log into the
cloud console, and one by one,

00:15:14.663 --> 00:15:16.080
click the instances
and figure out

00:15:16.080 --> 00:15:17.540
what action you want to take.

00:15:17.540 --> 00:15:19.290
And so today, what I'm
excited to announce

00:15:19.290 --> 00:15:21.360
is that we're going
to be enabling

00:15:21.360 --> 00:15:25.240
programmatic access to these
rightsizing recommendations.

00:15:25.240 --> 00:15:27.640
So we call it the
Rightsizing API.

00:15:27.640 --> 00:15:30.840
And the idea here is you can
query the Rightsizing API,

00:15:30.840 --> 00:15:33.953
get a list of recommendations,
all programmatically,

00:15:33.953 --> 00:15:36.120
and then programmatically
decide if you want to take

00:15:36.120 --> 00:15:37.680
the recommendation or not.

00:15:37.680 --> 00:15:39.840
And then apply the rightsizing.

00:15:39.840 --> 00:15:44.790
And so that would be a stop,
a resize, and then a restart.

00:15:44.790 --> 00:15:47.820
And then you can take that
program that you've written

00:15:47.820 --> 00:15:50.577
and schedule it so it
runs daily, or hourly,

00:15:50.577 --> 00:15:53.160
or whatever it might be to keep
up with the churn of instances

00:15:53.160 --> 00:15:55.200
that are coming on the fly.

00:15:55.200 --> 00:15:57.868
So that you can stay ahead
of all of the new instances

00:15:57.868 --> 00:15:59.410
that are getting
created so you don't

00:15:59.410 --> 00:16:00.785
have to keep
logging in every day

00:16:00.785 --> 00:16:03.460
and doing rightsizing manually.

00:16:03.460 --> 00:16:04.710
So it's pretty cool.

00:16:04.710 --> 00:16:07.650
It's in alpha today, and
it's coming to beta soon.

00:16:07.650 --> 00:16:12.810
So keep an eye out for it if
you find this interesting.

00:16:12.810 --> 00:16:15.870
So going back to
Example Inc, Example Inc

00:16:15.870 --> 00:16:18.780
realized that they've been
running 96 core VMs when they

00:16:18.780 --> 00:16:21.090
only really needed 72 core VMs.

00:16:21.090 --> 00:16:24.430
And they're shrinking the memory
in the same proportion as well.

00:16:24.430 --> 00:16:27.390
And as a result of that
change and rightsizing,

00:16:27.390 --> 00:16:32.107
they're able to save about
5% on their total bill.

00:16:32.107 --> 00:16:33.690
But they're not done
there yet, right?

00:16:33.690 --> 00:16:35.260
So they have a whole
bunch of other resources.

00:16:35.260 --> 00:16:36.968
So now they're moving
on, and now they're

00:16:36.968 --> 00:16:39.480
looking at some of the
attached resources that

00:16:39.480 --> 00:16:42.150
attached to the base VM, so
that second bucket of resources

00:16:42.150 --> 00:16:43.810
that we talked about.

00:16:43.810 --> 00:16:47.100
And so some of these resources
can be very expensive.

00:16:47.100 --> 00:16:49.230
So GPUs, local SSD,
these are examples

00:16:49.230 --> 00:16:51.420
of potentially very
expensive resources, where

00:16:51.420 --> 00:16:55.680
small amount of waste can accrue
to a very large amount of cost

00:16:55.680 --> 00:16:57.570
on your bill.

00:16:57.570 --> 00:17:02.220
And again, the powers of 2 are
striking here again, right?

00:17:02.220 --> 00:17:04.980
Compute Engine provides a
great deal more flexibility

00:17:04.980 --> 00:17:08.810
with GPUs than you'll
find with other providers.

00:17:08.810 --> 00:17:11.160
And so an example
here is, let's say

00:17:11.160 --> 00:17:17.210
you have a use case where
you need 64 core VM.

00:17:17.210 --> 00:17:19.710
In the power of 2 world,
there's a fixed ratio.

00:17:19.710 --> 00:17:23.730
If you want 64 cores, you
have to get eight V100 GPUs.

00:17:23.730 --> 00:17:25.500
But let's say you only need two.

00:17:25.500 --> 00:17:28.040
Well, you don't have that
option in the predefined world

00:17:28.040 --> 00:17:29.418
or the powers of 2 world.

00:17:29.418 --> 00:17:30.960
With Compute Engine,
you can go ahead

00:17:30.960 --> 00:17:36.570
and create that 64 core VM and
attach it to core V100 GPU.

00:17:36.570 --> 00:17:39.140
And there's significant
savings here, right?

00:17:39.140 --> 00:17:41.910
So just looking at the GPU
alone, it's $20 per hour

00:17:41.910 --> 00:17:44.910
versus $5 an hour.

00:17:44.910 --> 00:17:49.790
And so, Example Inc
took a look at this.

00:17:49.790 --> 00:17:54.560
And they realized that they
could shrink their right V100s

00:17:54.560 --> 00:17:57.400
to four V100s.

00:17:57.400 --> 00:18:00.300
And as a result, they
saved another around 5%.

00:18:00.300 --> 00:18:02.928
So with these two steps,
they're now saving about 10%

00:18:02.928 --> 00:18:04.220
on their total spend right now.

00:18:09.280 --> 00:18:12.320
So they're also using
persistent disk.

00:18:12.320 --> 00:18:15.130
And persistent disk is a
cheaper resource for sure.

00:18:15.130 --> 00:18:16.880
And you can have a lot
of persistent disk,

00:18:16.880 --> 00:18:19.340
and it doesn't add up to that
much, especially when you're

00:18:19.340 --> 00:18:22.970
comparing it to local
SSD, or GPUs, or even

00:18:22.970 --> 00:18:24.740
the base core and RAM.

00:18:24.740 --> 00:18:29.060
But a lot of VMs
have a lot of disks,

00:18:29.060 --> 00:18:31.970
and a lot of cloud workloads
have a lot of churn,

00:18:31.970 --> 00:18:35.240
where instances are
coming and going rapidly.

00:18:35.240 --> 00:18:37.790
And so one of the things that
we've seen from some customers

00:18:37.790 --> 00:18:41.630
is you end up in a situation
where instances get deleted,

00:18:41.630 --> 00:18:44.090
but persistent disks that
were attached to the instance

00:18:44.090 --> 00:18:45.200
don't get deleted.

00:18:45.200 --> 00:18:46.700
And so you end up
with a whole bunch

00:18:46.700 --> 00:18:49.330
of orphaned persistent
disks floating around.

00:18:49.330 --> 00:18:51.580
And you can imagine if you
don't pay attention to this

00:18:51.580 --> 00:18:55.310
for a long period of time,
and you have a lot of churn--

00:18:55.310 --> 00:18:57.320
instances are coming and going--

00:18:57.320 --> 00:18:59.930
you could end up with
thousands, hundreds or thousands

00:18:59.930 --> 00:19:00.800
of persistent disks.

00:19:00.800 --> 00:19:02.925
Or you might just start
hitting your quota limits--

00:19:02.925 --> 00:19:05.330
prevent you from going too high.

00:19:05.330 --> 00:19:08.570
So one tactical
thing you can do here

00:19:08.570 --> 00:19:11.780
is when you're creating
your disk, or your instance,

00:19:11.780 --> 00:19:13.710
or attaching the
disk to an instance,

00:19:13.710 --> 00:19:16.790
you can set a flag to
have it auto delete.

00:19:16.790 --> 00:19:18.950
And so when the
instance is deleted,

00:19:18.950 --> 00:19:21.650
the persistent disks that
you've marked this flag on

00:19:21.650 --> 00:19:22.970
will also get deleted.

00:19:22.970 --> 00:19:25.700
So it's a great way to prevent
this orphaning problem that

00:19:25.700 --> 00:19:28.050
could be happening.

00:19:28.050 --> 00:19:32.030
One caution on this is it's
a very powerful tool, right?

00:19:32.030 --> 00:19:33.560
It's a very, very
dangerous thing,

00:19:33.560 --> 00:19:35.360
like in the sense of
if you delete the VM

00:19:35.360 --> 00:19:37.670
and you actually want
the persistent disk,

00:19:37.670 --> 00:19:39.560
there's a bunch of
warnings that will pop up

00:19:39.560 --> 00:19:42.405
in the console, and in the cloud
interface, and all of that.

00:19:42.405 --> 00:19:43.780
But if you keep
going to the yes,

00:19:43.780 --> 00:19:46.490
yes, yes, that disk is
going to get deleted.

00:19:46.490 --> 00:19:48.590
And if you actually
do need the data,

00:19:48.590 --> 00:19:50.100
that's a really bad situation.

00:19:50.100 --> 00:19:53.030
So use this fly
cautiously, especially

00:19:53.030 --> 00:19:54.830
if you have different
people creating

00:19:54.830 --> 00:19:56.913
instances and different
people deleting instances.

00:19:56.913 --> 00:19:58.370
Then you may have
the right hand,

00:19:58.370 --> 00:20:00.297
left hand issue happening.

00:20:00.297 --> 00:20:02.630
But this is one thing that
we've seen customers leverage

00:20:02.630 --> 00:20:08.540
to kind of prevent excess
waste in terms of PD.

00:20:08.540 --> 00:20:12.120
So another thing you can do is
take advantage of snapshots.

00:20:12.120 --> 00:20:17.330
So snapshots are 85% cheaper
than a running PD SSD

00:20:17.330 --> 00:20:19.965
for the same amount of storage.

00:20:19.965 --> 00:20:21.590
And so with a snapshot,
what you can do

00:20:21.590 --> 00:20:25.700
is take a snapshot of
your disk and then delete

00:20:25.700 --> 00:20:27.235
the persistent disk.

00:20:27.235 --> 00:20:28.610
And the use case
there is, is you

00:20:28.610 --> 00:20:32.660
have some PDs just floating
around that you don't need.

00:20:32.660 --> 00:20:34.100
But you want the data.

00:20:34.100 --> 00:20:35.780
You're not attached
to any instance.

00:20:35.780 --> 00:20:37.610
So why not just
take the snapshot?

00:20:37.610 --> 00:20:39.090
Save the data.

00:20:39.090 --> 00:20:40.880
And then when you
need the data, you

00:20:40.880 --> 00:20:44.420
can rehydrate that
snapshot back into a disk,

00:20:44.420 --> 00:20:47.575
and then attach it to
a running instance.

00:20:47.575 --> 00:20:49.950
And then the final thing here
on sort of-- these are very

00:20:49.950 --> 00:20:54.210
tactical things, but the final
thing here is PD supports two

00:20:54.210 --> 00:20:56.480
types of disks--

00:20:56.480 --> 00:21:00.860
PD SSD, which is SSD,
and then PD standard.

00:21:00.860 --> 00:21:04.110
PD standard is significantly
cheaper than PD SSD.

00:21:04.110 --> 00:21:07.320
And not all workloads need the
performance characteristics

00:21:07.320 --> 00:21:09.090
of SSDs.

00:21:09.090 --> 00:21:10.560
So take a look at
your workloads,

00:21:10.560 --> 00:21:13.530
and identify where it
makes sense to use SSD

00:21:13.530 --> 00:21:15.570
and where it doesn't make sense.

00:21:15.570 --> 00:21:18.510
And convert over to PD
standard where possible.

00:21:22.160 --> 00:21:24.620
So we're going back
to Example Inc again.

00:21:24.620 --> 00:21:27.860
Example Inc took a look
at their 120 terabytes,

00:21:27.860 --> 00:21:32.610
and they realized that
about 14 terabytes of the PD

00:21:32.610 --> 00:21:35.390
SSD that they've provisioned--
which is basically because they

00:21:35.390 --> 00:21:37.348
had a whole bunch of
scripts, and everyone just

00:21:37.348 --> 00:21:40.100
launched their instances,
and they just got PD SSD.

00:21:40.100 --> 00:21:42.620
They realized that they
could cut 14 terabytes

00:21:42.620 --> 00:21:46.100
and convert that to
PD at a standard.

00:21:46.100 --> 00:21:50.520
So that results in like a 1%
to 2% increase in savings now.

00:21:50.520 --> 00:21:52.560
So smaller than the
larger instances,

00:21:52.560 --> 00:21:54.158
but that's kind of
the point, right?

00:21:54.158 --> 00:21:56.450
You're going to want to spend
your time on where you're

00:21:56.450 --> 00:21:59.143
spending the most money first to
get the best bang for your buck

00:21:59.143 --> 00:22:00.560
and then go down
that list of sort

00:22:00.560 --> 00:22:04.190
of the most expensive resource
all the way down to the least

00:22:04.190 --> 00:22:05.970
expensive resource.

00:22:05.970 --> 00:22:08.340
So now they're at 11%,
which is not bad, right?

00:22:08.340 --> 00:22:10.340
And we've been at this
for maybe 20 minutes now.

00:22:10.340 --> 00:22:13.310
We've saved them 11%
off of 1.5 million.

00:22:13.310 --> 00:22:15.180
Not bad.

00:22:15.180 --> 00:22:20.970
Another thing you can do is
leverage preemptible resources.

00:22:20.970 --> 00:22:24.660
And so preemptible
resources is basically

00:22:24.660 --> 00:22:26.520
our most discounted offering.

00:22:26.520 --> 00:22:31.060
It provides up to 80% off
of normal on demand prices.

00:22:31.060 --> 00:22:34.140
But it comes with some slightly
different characteristics.

00:22:34.140 --> 00:22:37.340
The resources act and look
just like a normal resource,

00:22:37.340 --> 00:22:39.240
so the performance
will be the same.

00:22:39.240 --> 00:22:41.230
Availability is the same.

00:22:41.230 --> 00:22:47.038
However, you can only keep
the resource for 24 hours.

00:22:47.038 --> 00:22:48.830
And there's a slight
chance that you may be

00:22:48.830 --> 00:22:52.420
preempted before the 24 hours.

00:22:52.420 --> 00:22:55.810
So you may be asking, whoa,
who would use this, right?

00:22:55.810 --> 00:22:58.720
If you have applications that
can checkpoint and tolerate

00:22:58.720 --> 00:23:00.610
sort of VMs coming and
going and restarting

00:23:00.610 --> 00:23:02.830
at a certain checkpoint,
this is a great way

00:23:02.830 --> 00:23:05.510
to save a lot of money on
some of your workloads.

00:23:05.510 --> 00:23:08.320
So some examples here are sort
of batch workloads, rendering.

00:23:08.320 --> 00:23:11.770
Things like that use
preemptible resources heavily.

00:23:15.360 --> 00:23:17.360
All right, so we're going
to switch to something

00:23:17.360 --> 00:23:19.296
a little more nichey
now, and we're

00:23:19.296 --> 00:23:21.720
going to talk about licensing.

00:23:21.720 --> 00:23:24.420
There's two types of licenses
I want to talk about today.

00:23:24.420 --> 00:23:27.922
The first is vCPU
based licenses.

00:23:27.922 --> 00:23:29.630
So you may have some
software that you've

00:23:29.630 --> 00:23:32.660
purchased that the software
provider says you can run it

00:23:32.660 --> 00:23:36.050
in the cloud, but you have to
license it based on each vCPU

00:23:36.050 --> 00:23:38.420
that you're running.

00:23:38.420 --> 00:23:42.410
You also may have some workloads
that are heavily memory bound

00:23:42.410 --> 00:23:43.882
and not compute bound.

00:23:43.882 --> 00:23:45.590
So databases are a
great example of this,

00:23:45.590 --> 00:23:49.880
where you need a whole
boatload of memory

00:23:49.880 --> 00:23:52.962
and not a lot of compute power.

00:23:52.962 --> 00:23:54.920
And so we have a feature
called extended memory

00:23:54.920 --> 00:23:58.130
that's directly addressed
at this type of use case.

00:23:58.130 --> 00:24:00.410
And the idea here is, is
that extended memory allows

00:24:00.410 --> 00:24:06.260
you to create VMs with much
higher memory to vCPU ratios

00:24:06.260 --> 00:24:08.540
than you can get
with custom machine

00:24:08.540 --> 00:24:11.070
types or predefined
machine types.

00:24:11.070 --> 00:24:14.300
So for example, you could
have a VM with one vCPU

00:24:14.300 --> 00:24:17.660
and 500 gigabytes of RAM.

00:24:17.660 --> 00:24:21.260
And so if you were to take that
use case with a vCPU license,

00:24:21.260 --> 00:24:24.440
you would be responsible to
pay for one vCPU license.

00:24:24.440 --> 00:24:25.910
But your workload
and your database

00:24:25.910 --> 00:24:31.180
would still have 500 gigabytes
of memory to work with.

00:24:31.180 --> 00:24:33.370
One thing I would
call out here is

00:24:33.370 --> 00:24:36.587
there is an extra cost
for using extended memory.

00:24:36.587 --> 00:24:38.170
So you're going to
want to do the math

00:24:38.170 --> 00:24:41.410
and say, what is the
cost per vCPU that I add?

00:24:41.410 --> 00:24:42.730
What is the cost?

00:24:42.730 --> 00:24:45.500
And then what is the cost
for enabling extended memory?

00:24:45.500 --> 00:24:46.450
And so, do that math.

00:24:46.450 --> 00:24:50.345
And many customers that I've
talked to for certain software

00:24:50.345 --> 00:24:52.720
licenses, extended memory
makes just a whole lot of sense

00:24:52.720 --> 00:24:57.125
because the vCPU licenses
dwarf the cost of the actual VM

00:24:57.125 --> 00:24:57.625
itself.

00:25:02.455 --> 00:25:04.330
Another type of license
that I want to talk--

00:25:04.330 --> 00:25:06.430
the second type of license
I want to talk about

00:25:06.430 --> 00:25:08.560
is server bound licenses.

00:25:08.560 --> 00:25:10.390
So these are
licenses that require

00:25:10.390 --> 00:25:14.050
you to license the physical
cores or physical sockets

00:25:14.050 --> 00:25:14.945
of a server.

00:25:14.945 --> 00:25:17.320
This is not something that
you could do on Compute Engine

00:25:17.320 --> 00:25:18.250
before.

00:25:18.250 --> 00:25:20.470
You may have noticed
in the keynote today,

00:25:20.470 --> 00:25:24.820
we announced support for bring
your own license on sole tenant

00:25:24.820 --> 00:25:26.120
nodes.

00:25:26.120 --> 00:25:28.150
And so the idea
here is we're now

00:25:28.150 --> 00:25:30.100
going to expose the
physical characteristics

00:25:30.100 --> 00:25:31.250
of our sole tenant nodes.

00:25:31.250 --> 00:25:35.540
So you'll know that there's
x sockets, y physical cores,

00:25:35.540 --> 00:25:38.320
which means now if you have some
of these server bound licenses,

00:25:38.320 --> 00:25:41.510
you can bring them onto
the sole tenant nodes.

00:25:41.510 --> 00:25:44.760
So Windows is a great example
of this, Windows Server.

00:25:44.760 --> 00:25:46.870
Windows server is a
great example of this.

00:25:46.870 --> 00:25:48.585
So before this
feature, if you wanted

00:25:48.585 --> 00:25:49.960
to run Windows on
Compute Engine,

00:25:49.960 --> 00:25:54.190
you would have to buy a
license from Compute Engine.

00:25:54.190 --> 00:25:57.585
And even though you may already
have Windows Server licenses

00:25:57.585 --> 00:25:59.710
that you purchased through
Microsoft or through one

00:25:59.710 --> 00:26:02.695
of their third party
authorized dealers--

00:26:02.695 --> 00:26:04.070
which is kind of
wasteful, right?

00:26:04.070 --> 00:26:05.410
You're basically paying
for two licenses.

00:26:05.410 --> 00:26:06.870
You can only use one of them.

00:26:06.870 --> 00:26:10.060
It doesn't seem super fair.

00:26:10.060 --> 00:26:13.780
So what you can do now is you
can take the licenses that you

00:26:13.780 --> 00:26:15.310
have on-prem or
that you've bought,

00:26:15.310 --> 00:26:18.070
and you can bring them
over to Compute Engine

00:26:18.070 --> 00:26:20.950
via this feature,
sole tenant BYOL.

00:26:20.950 --> 00:26:23.170
And so the example here is
these Microsoft licenses

00:26:23.170 --> 00:26:24.200
can be quite expensive.

00:26:24.200 --> 00:26:27.550
So just on the
server license alone,

00:26:27.550 --> 00:26:29.320
it's more than the
instance cost itself.

00:26:29.320 --> 00:26:32.860
So you could save more
than 55% on Compute Engine

00:26:32.860 --> 00:26:35.927
today if you just bring your
own licenses for Windows Server.

00:26:35.927 --> 00:26:37.510
But you could imagine
that this starts

00:26:37.510 --> 00:26:40.420
to get really,
really cheaper if you

00:26:40.420 --> 00:26:43.550
think that you're running, say,
Microsoft SQL Server on it as

00:26:43.550 --> 00:26:44.050
well.

00:26:48.000 --> 00:26:51.130
So if you remember, we talked
about Example Inc. Example

00:26:51.130 --> 00:26:54.530
Inc did have a small
footprint of Windows licenses.

00:26:54.530 --> 00:26:57.320
They had 192 Windows
core licenses

00:26:57.320 --> 00:27:02.250
that they were purchasing
through Compute Engine.

00:27:02.250 --> 00:27:04.400
With this new BYOL
feature, they basically

00:27:04.400 --> 00:27:06.620
spun up some sole tenant nodes.

00:27:06.620 --> 00:27:10.910
They launched their Windows
VMs into the sole tenant nodes.

00:27:10.910 --> 00:27:13.610
And as a result, they stopped
paying Google and Compute

00:27:13.610 --> 00:27:15.470
Engine for these
Windows licenses

00:27:15.470 --> 00:27:17.840
and instead, are able to
reuse the licenses that they

00:27:17.840 --> 00:27:19.850
have today.

00:27:19.850 --> 00:27:24.290
And the savings here add up
to another, I think, 3% to 4%.

00:27:24.290 --> 00:27:27.400
And so now we're up to about
15% total savings here.

00:27:31.250 --> 00:27:34.000
So we've gone through a whole
bunch of different things,

00:27:34.000 --> 00:27:37.960
in terms of being able to
reduce your bill from a resource

00:27:37.960 --> 00:27:38.600
perspective.

00:27:38.600 --> 00:27:40.360
And so the whole
point of this is

00:27:40.360 --> 00:27:43.840
to take all of the waste that's
happening with the resources

00:27:43.840 --> 00:27:46.840
that you're running and bring
it down to just exactly what

00:27:46.840 --> 00:27:47.865
you need, right?

00:27:47.865 --> 00:27:49.240
And there'll be
some noise there,

00:27:49.240 --> 00:27:51.580
but that's the whole point
of that section three,

00:27:51.580 --> 00:27:54.153
is to figure out how do you
shrink this down so that what

00:27:54.153 --> 00:27:55.570
you're using and
what you're being

00:27:55.570 --> 00:27:59.290
charged for is the minimum
amount that you actually need.

00:27:59.290 --> 00:28:01.930
Once you do that, then
it's time to start

00:28:01.930 --> 00:28:04.750
thinking about, OK, now I've got
this minimum amount of usage.

00:28:04.750 --> 00:28:08.260
How do I optimize the
discounts available to me?

00:28:12.592 --> 00:28:14.800
So the first one, we've
talked a little bit about it,

00:28:14.800 --> 00:28:17.490
but it's super simple and easy.

00:28:17.490 --> 00:28:19.960
It's sustained use discounts,
and the basic premise here

00:28:19.960 --> 00:28:23.380
is the more you run,
the more you save.

00:28:23.380 --> 00:28:32.268
Up to 30% off if you run the
resource for the entire month.

00:28:32.268 --> 00:28:34.060
And this is something
that we've continued.

00:28:34.060 --> 00:28:36.470
This is something that we
launched many years ago,

00:28:36.470 --> 00:28:39.660
and we've continued to
invest in it to try and make

00:28:39.660 --> 00:28:42.180
it more flexible and more fair.

00:28:42.180 --> 00:28:44.100
And so today, what
you can do now

00:28:44.100 --> 00:28:46.560
is you can start
and stop VMs freely.

00:28:46.560 --> 00:28:48.270
You can change the
shape of your VMs,

00:28:48.270 --> 00:28:52.520
so you can go from n1
standard 4 to n1 standard 16.

00:28:52.520 --> 00:28:54.270
The sustained use
discount will continue

00:28:54.270 --> 00:28:58.050
to accrue on a core and RAM
basis, independent of those two

00:28:58.050 --> 00:28:58.930
things.

00:28:58.930 --> 00:29:01.810
And you can change
zones within a region.

00:29:01.810 --> 00:29:04.933
So this gives you a whole
boatload of flexibility

00:29:04.933 --> 00:29:06.100
with sustained use discount.

00:29:06.100 --> 00:29:07.808
So you can run these
different instances.

00:29:07.808 --> 00:29:09.478
Things can change over time.

00:29:09.478 --> 00:29:11.770
And at the end of the month,
we'll look at what you ran

00:29:11.770 --> 00:29:14.550
and if you ran a resource
across different zones

00:29:14.550 --> 00:29:16.840
in the same region and
different VM types.

00:29:16.840 --> 00:29:21.360
But if that core ran for
a full 30 days or 31 days,

00:29:21.360 --> 00:29:22.427
you'll get 30% off.

00:29:22.427 --> 00:29:24.510
And so we're going to do
that calculation for you,

00:29:24.510 --> 00:29:25.680
and it's only fair, right?

00:29:25.680 --> 00:29:27.763
We're going to give you
the optimal discount based

00:29:27.763 --> 00:29:30.940
on the usage that
you're getting.

00:29:30.940 --> 00:29:32.500
So a little bit of
complexity that I

00:29:32.500 --> 00:29:35.590
want to add here that
has been recently added,

00:29:35.590 --> 00:29:39.280
is we do split sustained use
discount into two buckets

00:29:39.280 --> 00:29:40.082
today.

00:29:40.082 --> 00:29:42.040
So we have something
called the general purpose

00:29:42.040 --> 00:29:44.140
bucket and the memory
optimized bucket.

00:29:44.140 --> 00:29:48.400
So general purpose VMs are the
n1 standard, the n1 highmem,

00:29:48.400 --> 00:29:51.340
and the n1 highcpu, as well
as custom machine types

00:29:51.340 --> 00:29:53.080
and sole tenant nodes.

00:29:53.080 --> 00:29:55.280
And memory optimized are
memory optimized VMs.

00:29:55.280 --> 00:29:59.530
So these are n1 megamem
and n1 ultramem.

00:29:59.530 --> 00:30:01.240
And so what I mean by
separate buckets is

00:30:01.240 --> 00:30:03.430
that when I was saying
that you could change

00:30:03.430 --> 00:30:06.790
VM shapes, if you were to change
between a general purpose VM

00:30:06.790 --> 00:30:09.490
and a memory optimized VM,
the sustained use discounts

00:30:09.490 --> 00:30:10.240
don't stack.

00:30:10.240 --> 00:30:12.550
These are two different
pools or usage graphs

00:30:12.550 --> 00:30:14.800
that we look at to
optimize in terms of cost.

00:30:17.950 --> 00:30:20.805
So I think an example might
make this a little clearer.

00:30:20.805 --> 00:30:21.680
So here's an example.

00:30:21.680 --> 00:30:24.930
You're running an n1
standard 4 for half a month.

00:30:24.930 --> 00:30:26.920
And then there's some event.

00:30:26.920 --> 00:30:30.090
You get a whole bunch
of new traffic pop in.

00:30:30.090 --> 00:30:34.920
And as a result, you increase
the size of your VM 2 and n1

00:30:34.920 --> 00:30:35.670
standard 16.

00:30:38.180 --> 00:30:41.160
So it's only fair you ran four
cores for the entire month,

00:30:41.160 --> 00:30:41.880
right?

00:30:41.880 --> 00:30:44.990
So sustained use counts
will give you 30% discount

00:30:44.990 --> 00:30:46.580
on those four cores.

00:30:46.580 --> 00:30:49.160
And for the remaining
12 cores that are there,

00:30:49.160 --> 00:30:51.770
that got added as part
of the n1 standard

00:30:51.770 --> 00:30:54.300
16, that ran for half a month.

00:30:54.300 --> 00:30:55.850
So you'll get
around 10% discount,

00:30:55.850 --> 00:31:00.070
which is the half the
month type discount.

00:31:00.070 --> 00:31:03.280
So going along, so now if we
have to separate sustained use

00:31:03.280 --> 00:31:06.100
buckets, let's say you
started with an n1 standard 4

00:31:06.100 --> 00:31:09.750
and then you converted
to a n1 megamem--

00:31:09.750 --> 00:31:11.290
whatever.

00:31:11.290 --> 00:31:14.320
So different
sustained use buckets.

00:31:14.320 --> 00:31:18.040
In that scenario, you'd
only get half a month

00:31:18.040 --> 00:31:20.890
sustained use discount for the
n1 standard and then a half

00:31:20.890 --> 00:31:23.950
a month discount
for the megamem.

00:31:23.950 --> 00:31:25.972
So you'd only get
the 10% discount.

00:31:25.972 --> 00:31:27.430
Those four cores
won't get the 30%.

00:31:29.863 --> 00:31:31.030
So something to be aware of.

00:31:31.030 --> 00:31:32.340
So there's some
restrictions in terms

00:31:32.340 --> 00:31:34.340
of the different instances
that you change, too.

00:31:34.340 --> 00:31:37.610
There are some restrictions
that are happening there.

00:31:37.610 --> 00:31:39.550
So sustained use
discounts is pretty cool

00:31:39.550 --> 00:31:43.450
because you don't have to
send any signal to Google

00:31:43.450 --> 00:31:44.950
and say, hey, I'm
going to run this.

00:31:44.950 --> 00:31:47.320
And you still get
up to a 30% discount

00:31:47.320 --> 00:31:51.005
if you happen to run
for the entire month.

00:31:51.005 --> 00:31:52.630
But in some use cases,
you may actually

00:31:52.630 --> 00:31:54.670
know that you have
some steady state

00:31:54.670 --> 00:31:57.670
workloads that are going to run
for one or three years or even

00:31:57.670 --> 00:31:58.533
longer.

00:31:58.533 --> 00:31:59.950
And in those
scenarios, you should

00:31:59.950 --> 00:32:02.398
be able to get an even lower
price or a bigger discount.

00:32:02.398 --> 00:32:04.690
And so for that, we have
something called committed use

00:32:04.690 --> 00:32:05.710
discounts.

00:32:05.710 --> 00:32:07.480
And the idea with
committed use discounts

00:32:07.480 --> 00:32:10.810
is in exchange for a one
or three year commitment,

00:32:10.810 --> 00:32:13.160
you get a deeply
discounted price.

00:32:13.160 --> 00:32:16.000
And so for one year, you'll get
37% off, and for three year,

00:32:16.000 --> 00:32:18.213
you get 55% off.

00:32:18.213 --> 00:32:20.380
And we've tried to make
committed use discounts very

00:32:20.380 --> 00:32:22.172
similar to sustained
use discounts in terms

00:32:22.172 --> 00:32:24.620
of flexibility and fairness.

00:32:24.620 --> 00:32:26.530
So with a committed
use discount,

00:32:26.530 --> 00:32:28.340
you tell us in
aggregate what you want.

00:32:28.340 --> 00:32:32.470
So you could say, I want
10,000 cores and 5,000

00:32:32.470 --> 00:32:36.085
or 50,000 gigabytes of RAM.

00:32:36.085 --> 00:32:37.960
You don't have to tell
us the machine shapes,

00:32:37.960 --> 00:32:40.300
or specific zones,
or operating systems.

00:32:40.300 --> 00:32:42.730
And so as you start
spinning up those VMs,

00:32:42.730 --> 00:32:45.550
if things have to change-- those
VMs shut down and come back,

00:32:45.550 --> 00:32:48.460
or you change instance types
within the general purpose

00:32:48.460 --> 00:32:50.990
pool or the memory
optimized pool,

00:32:50.990 --> 00:32:53.030
the discounts will just accrue.

00:32:53.030 --> 00:32:55.280
And another use case
that we've seen is--

00:32:55.280 --> 00:32:56.860
and I think it's
pretty neat-- is

00:32:56.860 --> 00:32:59.770
that when you get to
really large scale,

00:32:59.770 --> 00:33:02.290
although any single
user or project may have

00:33:02.290 --> 00:33:04.210
a lot of volatility,
when you start

00:33:04.210 --> 00:33:07.187
to add a huge amount of projects
and a huge amount of usage,

00:33:07.187 --> 00:33:09.520
these customers will start
to notice that there are sort

00:33:09.520 --> 00:33:12.640
of these low watermarks that
they can buy committed use

00:33:12.640 --> 00:33:13.500
discounts out.

00:33:13.500 --> 00:33:16.360
So they're not actually
buying for a specific workload

00:33:16.360 --> 00:33:17.782
that they know is steady.

00:33:17.782 --> 00:33:19.990
They just know that in
aggregate across all the noise

00:33:19.990 --> 00:33:21.865
within the organization,
they're always going

00:33:21.865 --> 00:33:23.490
to have 50,000 cores running.

00:33:23.490 --> 00:33:26.387
And they may burst 100,000
cores at any point,

00:33:26.387 --> 00:33:28.720
but they know that they're
never going to go below that.

00:33:28.720 --> 00:33:30.178
So it's an ideal
thing to purchase.

00:33:34.930 --> 00:33:38.227
So committed use
discounts is maybe

00:33:38.227 --> 00:33:39.810
one of the most
popular pricing models

00:33:39.810 --> 00:33:43.737
we've seen on Compute Engine
for the last couple of years.

00:33:43.737 --> 00:33:45.570
And so today, I'm really
excited-- something

00:33:45.570 --> 00:33:46.562
I have been working on.

00:33:46.562 --> 00:33:48.270
I'm really excited to
announce that we're

00:33:48.270 --> 00:33:50.510
expanding committed
use discounts

00:33:50.510 --> 00:33:51.760
to additional resources.

00:33:51.760 --> 00:33:57.770
So we're going to support
GPUs, as well as local SSD.

00:33:57.770 --> 00:34:02.070
And so, it works very similar
to committed use discounts

00:34:02.070 --> 00:34:03.180
for cores and RAM.

00:34:03.180 --> 00:34:04.980
So you can do a one
year or a three year.

00:34:04.980 --> 00:34:08.159
And you'll get the similar
discounts, 37% for one year,

00:34:08.159 --> 00:34:11.370
55% for a three year.

00:34:11.370 --> 00:34:14.400
And we're going to support
all of the GPU types

00:34:14.400 --> 00:34:15.219
that we have today.

00:34:15.219 --> 00:34:21.630
So the K80, the P4,
the P100, and the V100.

00:34:21.630 --> 00:34:24.710
And the T4 is something that
we announced, I think, today.

00:34:24.710 --> 00:34:26.310
So that's coming
very soon, and we'll

00:34:26.310 --> 00:34:28.060
be supporting that as well.

00:34:28.060 --> 00:34:31.530
So if you are running
GPU workloads that

00:34:31.530 --> 00:34:33.570
have some sort of
steady state workload,

00:34:33.570 --> 00:34:35.387
this is a great way
to save some money.

00:34:35.387 --> 00:34:36.929
So if you know you're
going to run it

00:34:36.929 --> 00:34:38.613
for three years
or even one year,

00:34:38.613 --> 00:34:39.780
it makes sense to buy these.

00:34:44.550 --> 00:34:46.050
I'm also excited--
this is something

00:34:46.050 --> 00:34:47.133
I've also been working on.

00:34:47.133 --> 00:34:49.710
I'm also excited to introduce
a brand new billable resource

00:34:49.710 --> 00:34:52.800
that you have to worry about.

00:34:52.800 --> 00:34:55.290
They're called
zonal reservations.

00:34:55.290 --> 00:34:59.340
And the idea here is they
provide peace of mind

00:34:59.340 --> 00:35:03.060
that resources will
be available for you

00:35:03.060 --> 00:35:06.450
when and where you want them.

00:35:06.450 --> 00:35:08.080
And so the idea
here is you can say,

00:35:08.080 --> 00:35:13.830
I need to make sure that I
always have 101 standard 32's

00:35:13.830 --> 00:35:14.980
in US central 1a.

00:35:14.980 --> 00:35:17.910
So that's a specific zone
and a specific machine shape.

00:35:17.910 --> 00:35:19.573
You can create
these reservations.

00:35:19.573 --> 00:35:21.240
And so long as we
grant the reservation,

00:35:21.240 --> 00:35:23.840
you now know that those
resources are there for you.

00:35:23.840 --> 00:35:25.770
And you can convert
them into running

00:35:25.770 --> 00:35:27.340
instances whenever you want.

00:35:27.340 --> 00:35:29.340
And there's a bunch of
different use cases here.

00:35:29.340 --> 00:35:31.445
Some of the use cases
are for example,

00:35:31.445 --> 00:35:32.820
let's say you want
to do a patch.

00:35:32.820 --> 00:35:34.650
And so you're patching, and
you're doing a rolling patch.

00:35:34.650 --> 00:35:36.173
And some instances get deleted.

00:35:36.173 --> 00:35:38.340
You want to make sure that
you have enough resources

00:35:38.340 --> 00:35:40.340
to make sure that you can
complete the operation

00:35:40.340 --> 00:35:43.200
and come back in
a healthy state.

00:35:43.200 --> 00:35:46.263
So that's one of the things
that's going on there.

00:35:46.263 --> 00:35:47.680
So one of the
things that we tried

00:35:47.680 --> 00:35:50.250
to do really hard
with reservations

00:35:50.250 --> 00:35:52.000
was, again, live by
those three principles

00:35:52.000 --> 00:35:54.620
that we talked about--
simplicity, flexibility,

00:35:54.620 --> 00:35:56.480
and fairness.

00:35:56.480 --> 00:35:58.960
And so these reservations
are effectively just

00:35:58.960 --> 00:36:02.420
like running instances, from
Compute Engine's perspective.

00:36:02.420 --> 00:36:06.430
And so any of the discounts
you may have purchased already,

00:36:06.430 --> 00:36:09.340
like a committed use discount
or even sustained use discounts,

00:36:09.340 --> 00:36:12.038
should just apply like
it's a running instance.

00:36:12.038 --> 00:36:14.080
So if you purchased some
committed use discounts,

00:36:14.080 --> 00:36:16.870
you can imagine that
these reservations look

00:36:16.870 --> 00:36:19.960
and act just like an instance
from a discounting perspective.

00:36:19.960 --> 00:36:23.157
So if you happen to say, I
bought a CUD for this running

00:36:23.157 --> 00:36:25.240
instance, and I now want
to make it a reservation,

00:36:25.240 --> 00:36:28.030
you can do that, and the
committed use discounts

00:36:28.030 --> 00:36:29.560
will still work.

00:36:29.560 --> 00:36:32.260
And so similarly, on the
sustained use perspective,

00:36:32.260 --> 00:36:34.250
sustained use
discount perspective,

00:36:34.250 --> 00:36:37.540
we'll have reservations
for general purpose VMs,

00:36:37.540 --> 00:36:39.450
as well as memory optimized VMs.

00:36:39.450 --> 00:36:41.200
And so when you create
those reservations,

00:36:41.200 --> 00:36:43.420
they'll feed into those two
different sustained use buckets

00:36:43.420 --> 00:36:44.295
that we talked about.

